1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Welcome to Topic 8.

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:05,450
4, the last topic about storage and file
services.

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:10,100
You will meet three tools that every file
server administrator uses.

4
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:15,550
First, File Server Resource Manager, or
FSRM, which limits how much data users

5
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:19,400
store and which kinds of files they may
save.

6
00:00:19,650 --> 00:00:25,250
Second, iSCSI, which gives a server a disk
over the network, from another Windows

7
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:27,900
server or from a NAS appliance.

8
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:31,750
Third, Data Deduplication, which stores
repeated data only once.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,600
By the end you can set them up with the
GUI and with PowerShell.

10
00:00:37,850 --> 00:00:43,050
File Server Resource Manager is a role
service of File and Storage Services.

11
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:49,300
It answers two questions about a folder:
how much may users store, and what may

12
00:00:49,550 --> 00:00:50,750
they store.

13
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:52,600
Quota management limits space.

14
00:00:52,850 --> 00:00:54,850
File screening controls file types.

15
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:59,500
Storage reports show you usage, large
files, duplicates and screening attempts.

16
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:04,150
Classification and file management tasks
are concept only in this course.

17
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:09,200
One limit to remember: FSRM works on NTFS
volumes only, not ReFS.

18
00:01:09,450 --> 00:01:13,850
You install it with Install-WindowsFeature
FS-Resource-Manager and open it from the

19
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:15,700
Server Manager Tools menu.

20
00:01:15,950 --> 00:01:19,550
A quota sets a space limit on a folder.

21
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:25,400
A hard quota enforces it: once the folder
is full, nobody can save more.

22
00:01:25,650 --> 00:01:31,650
A soft quota lets users go over and only
sends notifications, so it is for

23
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:33,100
monitoring.

24
00:01:33,350 --> 00:01:38,150
Notification thresholds fire at a
percentage, for example a warning email at

25
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:39,600
85 percent.

26
00:01:39,850 --> 00:01:44,650
Quota templates save settings for reuse,
like the built-in 100 MB Limit.

27
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:50,900
An auto apply quota sits on a parent
folder and gives every existing and new

28
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:52,750
subfolder its own quota.

29
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Here is the Create Quota dialog in the
FSRM console.

30
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:01,250
In Quota path, type the folder, C colon
Shares Projects.

31
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:06,300
Keep Create quota on path, so the quota
covers this one folder.

32
00:02:06,550 --> 00:02:11,750
You can derive the settings from a
template, or select Define custom quota

33
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,000
properties and then Custom Properties.

34
00:02:14,250 --> 00:02:19,450
In the Quota Properties dialog, under
Space Limit, type 100 and choose MB.

35
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:24,100
Then select Hard quota, Do not allow users
to exceed limit.

36
00:02:24,350 --> 00:02:26,350
Select OK and then Create.

37
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:30,600
For one folder, skip saving the settings
as a template.

38
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:33,650
This is the File Screen Properties dialog.

39
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:38,700
Under Screening type, active screening
blocks the files: the user gets Access

40
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:40,150
denied.

41
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:44,400
Passive screening lets the save happen and
only notifies you.

42
00:02:44,650 --> 00:02:47,850
Under file groups, you pick what to block.

43
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:54,500
A file group is a named list of patterns,
and Audio and Video Files includes star

44
00:02:54,750 --> 00:02:58,750
dot mp3, star dot mp4, star dot wav and
more.

45
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:04,600
And if one subfolder needs audio files
after all, a file screen exception allows

46
00:03:04,850 --> 00:03:06,450
that group again there.

47
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:10,700
The same work in PowerShell takes only a
few lines.

48
00:03:10,950 --> 00:03:13,350
Install-WindowsFeature adds FSRM with its
tools.

49
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:19,200
New-FsrmQuota with dash Path and dash Size
100MB creates a hard quota, because you

50
00:03:19,450 --> 00:03:21,450
did not add dash SoftLimit.

51
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:24,900
To use a template instead, add dash
Template.

52
00:03:25,150 --> 00:03:29,950
New-FsrmFileScreen with dash IncludeGroup
Audio and Video Files and the switch dash

53
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:32,200
Active creates the active screen.

54
00:03:32,450 --> 00:03:35,250
Get-FsrmFileGroup shows the patterns in a
group.

55
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:39,900
Finally, Get-FsrmQuota shows the size in
bytes, 104857600, and SoftLimit False,

56
00:03:40,150 --> 00:03:42,950
which tells you the quota is enforced.

57
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:46,400
Now to storage networking, a Server plus
topic.

58
00:03:46,650 --> 00:03:51,050
A storage area network, or SAN, gives a
server block storage.

59
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:56,100
The server sees a raw disk and creates the
file system itself.

60
00:03:56,350 --> 00:04:00,750
iSCSI sends disk commands over ordinary
Ethernet, on TCP port 3260.

61
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,800
The initiator is the client, here SRV01.

62
00:04:04,050 --> 00:04:08,850
The target is the storage server, here
SRV-CORE, offering the target sa1-target.

63
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:14,300
A LUN, a logical unit number, is one disk
that a target offers.

64
00:04:14,550 --> 00:04:21,350
Compare that with a NAS, where you get a
file share and the NAS owns the file

65
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:22,800
system.

66
00:04:23,050 --> 00:04:27,450
Every initiator and target has an iSCSI
Qualified Name, or IQN.

67
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:33,700
SRV01's IQN starts with iqn, then the year
and month the naming authority owned its

68
00:04:33,950 --> 00:04:39,550
domain, then that domain reversed, com dot
microsoft, and after the colon a unique

69
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:41,000
name.

70
00:04:41,250 --> 00:04:46,050
A portal is the IP address and port where
a target listens.

71
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:49,100
A Windows target lists the allowed
initiators.

72
00:04:49,350 --> 00:04:55,350
CHAP adds a shared secret, and mutual CHAP
checks both sides; our lab uses none.

73
00:04:55,600 --> 00:05:01,600
In production, iSCSI runs on its own
network or VLAN, and MPIO adds a second

74
00:05:01,850 --> 00:05:03,050
path.

75
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:07,700
SRV-CORE is Server Core, so you build the
target in PowerShell.

76
00:05:07,950 --> 00:05:09,950
Install-WindowsFeature
FS-iSCSITarget-Server adds the role

77
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:11,800
service, with no restart.

78
00:05:12,050 --> 00:05:17,650
Create a folder, then New-IscsiVirtualDisk
makes C colon iSCSI lun1 dot vhdx, 5 GB.

79
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:23,100
Without dash UseFixed the file is dynamic
and grows as data is written.

80
00:05:23,350 --> 00:05:26,950
New-IscsiServerTarget creates sa1-target,
and dash InitiatorIds allows only SRV01's

81
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:28,400
IQN.

82
00:05:28,650 --> 00:05:32,650
Add-IscsiVirtualDiskTargetMapping maps the
disk to the target as LUN 0.

83
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:36,900
Get-IscsiServerTarget then shows the
target, its IQN and the status

84
00:05:37,150 --> 00:05:39,150
NotConnected, until SRV01 logs on.

85
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:45,000
On SRV01 you open the iSCSI Initiator,
from Server Manager Tools or by running

86
00:05:45,250 --> 00:05:46,450
iscsicpl.

87
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:51,100
The first time, Windows offers to start
the Microsoft iSCSI service.

88
00:05:51,350 --> 00:05:52,550
Select Yes.

89
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:58,800
On the Targets tab, Quick Connect finds
the targets at an address and logs on.

90
00:05:59,050 --> 00:06:05,050
You can also add the portal on the
Discovery tab, then select a target and

91
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:06,500
Connect.

92
00:06:06,750 --> 00:06:12,350
The Connect To Target dialog can add the
connection to Favorite Targets, so it

93
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:14,200
returns after a restart.

94
00:06:14,450 --> 00:06:16,850
Connected means a new disk appears.

95
00:06:17,100 --> 00:06:21,100
The Configuration tab shows this server's
initiator name, its IQN.

96
00:06:21,350 --> 00:06:24,150
Here is the initiator side in PowerShell.

97
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:26,400
The service is called MSiSCSI.

98
00:06:26,650 --> 00:06:30,250
Set it to start automatically and start it
now.

99
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:34,900
New-IscsiTargetPortal adds SRV-CORE's
address as a portal, so discovery runs on

100
00:06:35,150 --> 00:06:36,350
TCP port 3260.

101
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:41,800
Get-IscsiTarget lists what the portal
offers, and you pick the target whose name

102
00:06:42,050 --> 00:06:43,250
contains sa1-target.

103
00:06:43,500 --> 00:06:47,500
Connect-IscsiTarget logs on, and dash
IsPersistent true makes the connection

104
00:06:47,750 --> 00:06:49,750
come back after every restart.

105
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,600
Get-IscsiSession confirms it: IsConnected
and IsPersistent are both True.

106
00:06:53,850 --> 00:06:57,850
Forget IsPersistent and your disk
disappears at the next reboot.

107
00:06:58,100 --> 00:07:03,300
A NAS appliance like NAS01, running
TrueNAS, can also be an iSCSI target.

108
00:07:03,550 --> 00:07:05,550
Same steps, other portal: 192.

109
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:07,000
168.

110
00:07:07,250 --> 00:07:08,450
50.

111
00:07:08,700 --> 00:07:09,900
40.

112
00:07:10,150 --> 00:07:16,150
TrueNAS sets its own IQN prefix, so you
match the target by its name, sa1-nas.

113
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:22,400
On Windows Server, new disks on a shared
bus often arrive offline, because of the

114
00:07:22,650 --> 00:07:23,850
SAN policy.

115
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:30,100
Pick the right disk by its size, 10 GB
here, never by its number alone.

116
00:07:30,350 --> 00:07:36,350
Bring it online, initialize it as GPT,
create a partition N and format it NTFS

117
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:38,200
with the label NAS.

118
00:07:38,450 --> 00:07:40,450
Look at the three files.

119
00:07:40,700 --> 00:07:45,100
Each is cut into chunks, and many chunks
are the same.

120
00:07:45,350 --> 00:07:50,150
The chunk store, in System Volume
Information, keeps each unique chunk once,

121
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:51,600
optionally compressed.

122
00:07:51,850 --> 00:07:55,850
Here twelve chunks of data need only five
stored chunks.

123
00:07:56,100 --> 00:08:00,500
The files now hold reparse points that
point to their chunks.

124
00:08:00,750 --> 00:08:05,550
It is post-processing: files are written
normally, and a scheduled job optimizes

125
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:07,000
them later.

126
00:08:07,250 --> 00:08:13,250
It works on data volumes, NTFS or ReFS,
never on the system or boot volume.

127
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:16,700
Server 2025 also has separate ReFS native
dedup.

128
00:08:16,950 --> 00:08:20,950
In Server Manager, go to File and Storage
Services, Volumes.

129
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:25,600
Right-click the data volume E colon and
select Configure Data Deduplication.

130
00:08:25,850 --> 00:08:28,650
In the dialog, choose the usage type.

131
00:08:28,900 --> 00:08:33,700
General purpose file server is the usage
type called Default in PowerShell.

132
00:08:33,950 --> 00:08:38,350
Virtual Desktop Infrastructure server is
HyperV, and Virtualized Backup Server is

133
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:39,800
Backup.

134
00:08:40,050 --> 00:08:44,050
Next, Deduplicate files older than, in
days, defaults to 3.

135
00:08:44,300 --> 00:08:49,500
In the lab you set 0 so you can see
results right away.

136
00:08:49,750 --> 00:08:55,350
You can exclude file types and folders,
and the schedule button sets when jobs

137
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:56,800
run.

138
00:08:57,050 --> 00:08:59,850
In PowerShell, Install-WindowsFeature
FS-Data-Deduplication adds the feature.

139
00:09:00,100 --> 00:09:04,500
Enable-DedupVolume with dash UsageType
Default turns it on for E colon.

140
00:09:04,750 --> 00:09:08,350
Set-DedupVolume with dash
MinimumFileAgeDays 0 is the lab shortcut.

141
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:13,800
Then Start-DedupJob, type Optimization,
with dash Wait, runs a job now and shows

142
00:09:14,050 --> 00:09:15,250
progress.

143
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:19,900
Get-DedupStatus and Get-DedupVolume show
saved space, optimized files and the usage

144
00:09:20,150 --> 00:09:21,350
type.

145
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:24,400
Your numbers will differ from anyone
else's.

146
00:09:24,650 --> 00:09:30,250
Normally you do not start jobs by hand:
optimization runs about every hour, and

147
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:32,900
garbage collection and scrubbing run
weekly.

148
00:09:33,150 --> 00:09:34,750
Four mistakes to avoid.

149
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:40,200
One: choosing a soft quota or passive
screening when you meant to block.

150
00:09:40,450 --> 00:09:44,450
Soft and passive only notify, so pick hard
and active.

151
00:09:44,700 --> 00:09:49,100
Two: connecting two servers to the same
LUN without a cluster.

152
00:09:49,350 --> 00:09:51,350
The file system gets corrupted.

153
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:54,800
List only one server's IQN on the target.

154
00:09:55,050 --> 00:09:58,250
Three: connecting a target without making
it persistent.

155
00:09:58,500 --> 00:10:01,700
After the next restart, the disk is gone.

156
00:10:01,950 --> 00:10:06,750
Four: expecting dedup to save space at
once, or on C colon.

157
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,000
It works on data volumes, after the
minimum file age.

158
00:10:11,250 --> 00:10:18,050
C colon Shares Projects has a 100 MB hard
quota and an active Audio and Video Files

159
00:10:18,300 --> 00:10:19,500
screen.

160
00:10:19,750 --> 00:10:25,350
First, the folder holds 90 MB and a user
saves a 20 MB file.

161
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:29,600
What happens, and what would happen with a
soft quota?

162
00:10:29,850 --> 00:10:33,050
Second, a user saves song dot mp3 there.

163
00:10:33,300 --> 00:10:36,500
What happens, and what would passive
screening do?

164
00:10:36,750 --> 00:10:42,750
Third, in our iSCSI setup, which side is
the initiator, and which port must reach

165
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:44,200
SRV-CORE?

166
00:10:44,450 --> 00:10:48,850
Fourth, why can you not enable Data
Deduplication on C colon?

167
00:10:49,100 --> 00:10:51,100
Pause and answer all four.

168
00:10:51,350 --> 00:10:58,150
One: with the hard quota, the save fails,
because 90 plus 20 is more than 100 MB.

169
00:10:58,400 --> 00:11:03,600
With a soft quota it would work, and you
would only get notifications.

170
00:11:03,850 --> 00:11:09,050
Two: active screening refuses song dot
mp3, and the user sees Access denied.

171
00:11:09,300 --> 00:11:12,500
Passive screening would save it and only
notify.

172
00:11:12,750 --> 00:11:18,750
Three: SRV01 is the initiator and SRV-CORE
is the target, so SRV01 must reach TCP

173
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:20,600
port 3260 at 192.

174
00:11:20,850 --> 00:11:22,050
168.

175
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:23,500
50.

176
00:11:23,750 --> 00:11:24,950
21.

177
00:11:25,200 --> 00:11:30,800
Four: C colon is the system and boot
volume, and deduplication only runs on

178
00:11:31,050 --> 00:11:33,450
data volumes such as E colon.

179
00:11:33,700 --> 00:11:34,900
Now the pod.

180
00:11:35,150 --> 00:11:41,950
On SRV01, I install FSRM and give the
Projects folder a 100 MB hard quota and an

181
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:44,200
active audio and video screen.

182
00:11:44,450 --> 00:11:50,850
Watch: the second 60 MB file fails with
not enough space, and song dot mp3 gets

183
00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:52,300
Access denied.

184
00:11:52,550 --> 00:11:58,150
On SRV-CORE, I build sa1-target with a 5
GB virtual disk for SRV01 only.

185
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:03,600
Back on SRV01, the iSCSI Initiator
discovers the portal and connects as a

186
00:12:03,850 --> 00:12:07,450
favorite target, and the new disk becomes
I colon.

187
00:12:07,700 --> 00:12:12,900
Last, I turn on deduplication for E colon
and run an optimization job.

188
00:12:13,150 --> 00:12:17,550
FSRM gives you quotas, file screens and
storage reports on NTFS.

189
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:23,400
Hard quotas block, soft quotas notify, and
templates and auto apply quotas save work.

190
00:12:23,650 --> 00:12:27,650
Active file screens block file groups;
passive screens only notify.

191
00:12:27,900 --> 00:12:33,500
iSCSI connects an initiator to a target
and its LUNs, identified by IQNs, through

192
00:12:33,750 --> 00:12:35,750
a portal on TCP 3260.

193
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:40,400
Make connections persistent, and expect
new SAN disks to arrive offline.

194
00:12:40,650 --> 00:12:45,450
Deduplication works on data volumes with a
chunk store and scheduled jobs.

195
00:12:45,700 --> 00:12:46,900
In Lab 8.

196
00:12:47,150 --> 00:12:52,350
4, about 90 minutes and practice only, you
build all of this yourself.
